{"userId":"Ft2lHPf0gIju3UTl","userEmail":"expert@dairylab.org","adulterantSlug":"water","adulterantName":"Excessive Water / Neutralizers","imageUrl":"/uploads/strip-1792406740853-842559088.png","sampledColorHex":"#371556","sampledColorRgb":[55,21,86],"detectedStatus":"ADULTERATED","confidenceScore":99,"distanceToPure":194,"distanceToAdulterated":0,"chemicalReport":{"purpose":"Used for volume dilution, which drastically lowers natural density and SNF; neutralizers mask acidity of stale milk.","snfImpact":"Directly drops milk Solids-Not-Fat below the statutory 8.5% (cow) and 9.0% (buffalo) standards.","densityImpact":"Lowers density well below 1.026 g/cm³ (Lactometer reading < 26 at 20°C).","chemicalMechanism":"Freezing point depression shift and Rosolic acid / Phenolphthalein pH indicator reaction with neutralizer alkalis.","healthHazards":["Severe child and maternal malnutrition due to diluted protein and calcium","Waterborne pathogens: Salmonella, E. coli, cholera, and Amoebiasis from contaminated tap or pond water","Heavy metal poisoning (lead, arsenic) from untreated water sources","Disruption of stomach acid balance by caustic alkaline neutralizers"],"fssaiLimit":"Added water must be 0%. Lactometer reading must not fall below 28 for cow milk and 30 for buffalo milk."},"notes":"Lactometer reading was 21","sampleName":"Water Dilution + Neutralizer Sample","createdAt":{"$$date":1792406740859},"_id":"KTuyXbmhT11vHDcL"}
